All font sources work. Now let me apply the modernization.

### 1. Font links + variables

Replace:
```html
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/>
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Grotesk:wght@300;400;500;600;700&family=Syne:wght@700;800;900&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/>
```
with:
```html
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net" crossorigin/>
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bricolage+Grotesque:opsz,wght@12..96,400;12..96,600;12..96,800&family=Inter:wght@300;400;500;600;700;800&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/>
<link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/orioncactus/pretendard@v1.3.9/dist/web/variable/pretendardvariable-dynamic-subset.min.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
```

CSS variables:
```css
--font: 'Inter', 'Pretendard', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, system-ui, sans-serif;
--font-head: 'Bricolage Grotesque', 'Pretendard', system-ui, sans-serif;
```

Note: `font-weight:900` is used in `.intro-headline`, `.r-dest-name`, `.load-h`. Bricolage Grotesque variable supports up to 800. Inter supports up to 800 (as requested). Let me request 800 max and change the 900s to 800. Actually, variable fonts: if I request `family=Inter:wght@300..800` the served font supports 300-800. Using `font-weight:900` would synthesize/pick 800 and could trigger faux bold in some browsers. Better to request up to 900 if available. Inter variable supports up to 900. Let me use `wght@300..900`.

Bricolage Grotesque variable supports 200-800. Let me request `opsz,wght@12..96,400..800`.

And change `font-weight:900` → `800` where the display font is used.

Let me check which elements use font-weight 900:
- `.intro-headline{font-weight:900;}`
- `.r-dest-name{font-weight:900;}`
- `.load-h{font-weight:900;}`
- `.bcl-total{font-weight:900;}` (uses --font, Inter)
- `.alt-pct-badge{font-weight:900;}` (--font)
- `.vs-badge{font-weight:900;}` (--font)
- `.qn-round{font-weight:800;}`
- `.cc-btn{font-weight:800;}`

Inter supports 900 so those are fine. For the display font (Bricolage), 900 → 800.

### 2. Background

Replace body background system.

New:
```css
:root{
  --bg:      #07070E;
  --bg2:     #0B0B16;
  ...
}

body{
  font-family:var(--font);
  background:var(--bg);
  color:var(--text);
  min-height:100vh;
  overflow-x:hidden;
  line-height:1.6;
  position:relative;
  -webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased;
  -moz-osx-font-smoothing:grayscale;
  text-rendering:optimizeLegibility;
}
body::before{   /* ambient mesh gradient */
  content:'';
  position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:0;pointer-events:none;
  background:
    radial-gradient(58% 48% at 10% 4%,   rgba(99,102,241,.20) 0%, transparent 62%),
    radial-gradient(52% 44% at 92% 8%,   rgba(56,189,248,.13) 0%, transparent 60%),
    radial-gradient(64% 56% at 50% 100%, rgba(168,85,247,.13) 0%, transparent 64%),
    linear-gradient(180deg,#07070E 0%,#0A0A15 42%,#08080F 100%);
}
body::after{    /* film grain */
  content:'';
  position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:0;pointer-events:none;
  opacity:.22;
  mix-blend-mode:overlay;
  background-image:url("data:image/svg+xml,...");
  background-size:200px 200px;
}
```

Hmm, `mix-blend-mode:overlay` with opacity .22 — the grain SVG is a grayscale turbulence. With overlay blend on a dark bg, it'll add subtle texture. Let me use `soft-light` with opacity .35, which is gentler and won't lighten much. Let me go with `overlay` and opacity .14. I'll test visually.

Actually a common approach: `opacity:.04` with `mix-blend-mode:overlay` and a white-noise SVG. Let me test both in the browser and pick.

Let me use: opacity .3, mix-blend-mode: soft-light, with the fractalNoise SVG. Should be subtle.

### 3. Blobs → modern aurora

Replace the 4 blobs with 2 large, soft, slowly drifting gradient orbs with lower opacity. Or remove entirely and rely on the mesh + grain.

Let me keep 3 but much subtler:
```css
.blob{
  position:absolute;border-radius:50%;
  filter:blur(100px);opacity:.16;
  animation:blobDrift ease-in-out infinite alternate;
}
.blob-1{width:640px;height:640px;background:radial-gradient(circle at 30% 30%, #6366F1, transparent 70%);top:-25%;left:-18%;animation-duration:26s;}
.blob-2{width:520px;height:520px;background:radial-gradient(circle at 60% 40%, #0EA5E9, transparent 70%);top:40%;right:-16%;animation-duration:32s;animation-delay:-10s;}
.blob-3{width:480px;height:480px;background:radial-gradient(circle at 50% 50%, #A855F7, transparent 70%);bottom:-18%;left:28%;animation-duration:36s;animation-delay:-18s;}
```
And remove blob-4. And slow the drift.

Actually — "modern" often means less decoration. The mesh gradient in body::before already provides the color. Having both mesh + blobs doubles the glow. Let me REMOVE the blobs entirely and put all the ambient color in the mesh gradient, plus grain. That's cleaner, faster, and more modern. 

But `.blobs` div is in the HTML. I'd remove the div and the CSS. Let me keep the div but empty? No — let me just remove the blobs markup and CSS, and remove the emoji particles markup + JS.

Hmm, but then the page might feel flat. Let me instead keep a single subtle aurora layer. I'll keep the `.blobs` container with 2 soft orbs that move slowly — gives life without the 4-way blob party.

Decision: 
- Keep 2 orbs, much softer (opacity .14), slower (28s/34s), and remove the 3rd/4th.
- Remove the emoji particles (replace with a subtle CSS-only "dust" — no, just remove).

Wait — removing the particles means `spawnParticles()` becomes dead. Let me remove the function and its call, and remove the `#ptcl` div.

Actually, a nice modern touch that replaces the emoji: a very subtle animated "shimmer" grid or a static fine noise. The grain handles texture. Let me just remove particles.

### 4. Glassmorphism refinement

Current:
```css
.glass-bg: rgba(255,255,255,0.05);
.glass-border: rgba(255,255,255,0.09);
.glass-shadow: 0 8px 32px rgba(0,0,0,0.45);
